<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thukela River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tugela River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thukela, or Tugela, is the largest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a>, plunges over the Tugela Falls and flows about 500 kilometres east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. In the nineteenth century it was the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a> and the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colony-of-natal">Colony of Natal</a>, and it saw heavy fighting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thukela-river.jpg" alt="Thukela River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bothar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphitheatre_Drakensberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Thukela, called the Tugela for most of the 20th century, is the largest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a> and one of the most important rivers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a> range at Mont-aux-Sources, the source also of the Orange and Caledon rivers, and flows through the KwaZulu-Natal midlands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. It drops 1 370 metres in its lower 480 kilometres, and its catchment covers about 29,100 square kilometres, where land use is mainly rural subsistence farming and commercial forestry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The largest of its tributaries is the Mzinyathi, or Buffalo, River, which joins about 19 kilometres east of Tugela Ferry. The others include the Little Tugela, Klip, Mooi, Blood, Sundays, Ingagani and Bushman rivers. The Blood River was named by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a>, after they defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> on 16 December 1838, when the river is said to have run red with the blood of Zulu warriors. Below it lies Rorke's Drift, a crossing point and the site of a battle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The scaly yellowfish, Labeobarbus natalensis, a common fish found only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, lives in the Tugela system. The spelling Tugela, an Anglicised form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> name Thukela, was used for most of the 20th century. Earlier writers used many other spellings: Isaacs in 1836 wrote Ootergale and Ootoogale, C. R. Maclean in 1853 wrote Zootagoola, and the artist George French Angas wrote Tugala. Some of the variation arose because early European writers did not know that Zulu grammar uses prefixes such as i- or u- to mark the class of a noun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tugela River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the start of the nineteenth century the Zulu were a small Nguni-speaking chiefdom on the White Mfolozi river, one of many in the land between the Phongolo and <b>Thukela</b> rivers in what is now KwaZulu-Natal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
